/** @page python_typesguide Type Conversions Between Qore and Python

    @tableofcontents

    @section python_qore_to_python Type Conversions From Qore To Python

    |!Source %Qore Type|!Target Python Type
    |\c binary|\c bytearray
    |\c bool|\c bool
    |\c date|\c datetime.datetime (absolute date/time values) or \c datetime.delta (relative date/time values)
    |\c float|\c float
    |\c hash|\c dict
    |\c int|\c int
    |\c list|\c list
    |\c string|\c str
    |\c code (closure or call reference)|\c callable

    @note \c datetime.datetime supports years 1 - 9999 only; converting an absolute %Qore date/time value with a
    year outside this range raises an exception, as the value has no %Python representation.  When such a value is
    a %Qore class constant being imported into %Python, the constant is skipped and the rest of the class is
    imported normally.

    @section python_python_to_qore Type Conversions From Python to Qore

    |!Source Python Type|!Target %Qore Type
    |\c bool|\c bool
    |\c int|\c int, or \c number outside the 64-bit signed integer range
    |\c float|\c float
    |\c bytes|\c binary
    |\c bytearray|\c binary
    |\c datetime.datetime|\c date (absolute date/time values)
    |\c datetime.delta|\c date (relative date/time values)
    |\c dict|\c hash
    |\c list|\c list
    |\c str|\c string
    |\c tuple|\c list
    |\c function, \c method|\c code

    All other %Python types are converted to a %Qore object using a dynamically-created class that wraps the %Python
    type or class.

    Values that a %Qore value cannot represent raise an exception:
    - a container (\c list, \c tuple or \c dict) that contains itself, directly or through other containers, raises
      a \c PYTHON-VALUE-ERROR exception; a container referenced more than once without a cycle is converted each time
    - a string (or a \c dict key) with lone surrogates, which cannot be encoded in UTF-8, raises the %Python
      \c UnicodeEncodeError exception (\c builtins.UnicodeEncodeError)
    - an \c int with more significant bits than the maximum precision of a %Qore \c number (8192 bits, about 2466
      decimal digits) raises a \c PYTHON-VALUE-ERROR exception, as it cannot be converted exactly
*/
